--- note
### The update rule, split into surprise × input — eq. (8)

$$
W_{t+1} = W_t - \eta_{t+1}\underbrace{\nabla_{W}\mathcal{L}(W_t;\boldsymbol{x}_{t+1})}_{\text{Surprise}} = W_t - \eta_{t+1}\underbrace{\nabla_{y_{t+1}}\mathcal{L}(W_t;\boldsymbol{x}_{t+1})}_{\text{Surprise in the output}}\otimes\,\boldsymbol{x}_{t+1}
$$

The factorisation that starts the paper's argument. Because `y = Wx`, the weight
gradient *is* the outer product of the **Local Surprise Signal** `u = ∇_y L` with the input. So a
training step writes the pair (key = input, value = its surprise) into `W`. `∇_y L` is zero exactly when
the loss is minimised, so the value being stored is the *error*.

--- code
W = torch.randn(3, 5, requires_grad=True); x = torch.randn(5); tgt = torch.randn(3)
y = W @ x; (0.5 * (y - tgt).pow(2).sum()).backward()
u = (y - tgt).detach()                                          # the Local Surprise Signal, dL/dy
ok("grad_W == (dL/dy) outer x  (eq. 8)", close(W.grad, torch.outer(u, x)))
ok("surprise vanishes at a perfect prediction", close((y - y).detach(), torch.zeros(3)))
print("so a training step is a memory write: key = x, value = -eta * surprise")

--- output
```
PASS  grad_W == (dL/dy) outer x  (eq. 8)
PASS  surprise vanishes at a perfect prediction
so a training step is a memory write: key = x, value = -eta * surprise
```

--- image
learning/assets/nested-learning/eq/eq_p08_021.png
